Use `getBy` when the element should already exist; it returns the match or throws immediately. Use `queryBy` when absence is the expected result, because it returns `null` instead of throwing. Use `findBy` when the element appears asynchronously after an interaction, state update, or request; it returns a promise and retries until the timeout. Choose the semantic query first, commonly `getByRole` with an accessible name or `getByLabelText` for a form control. Do not add a fixed sleep to handle asynchronous rendering; wait for the user-visible condition with a `findBy` query or the appropriate async helper.

```ts caption="Use absence queries for negative assertions and async queries for eventual UI."
expect(screen.queryByRole('alert')).not.toBeInTheDocument();
await expect(screen.findByRole('alert')).resolves.toBeInTheDocument();
```

A behavior test renders the component and performs actions through the same observable interface a user has, then checks visible output, enabled controls, navigation, or submitted data. It should not inspect component instances, private state, render counts, or internal helper calls merely because they are convenient. Testing Library explicitly favors DOM nodes and tests that resemble software use. That makes a refactor—such as replacing local state with a reducer—less likely to break a test when the user-visible contract is unchanged. Implementation-level assertions still have a place for a pure unit or a deliberately isolated adapter, but they should not be the default for UI behavior.

```ts caption="Assert the observable result, not the component's internal state."
await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /save/i }));
expect(screen.getByRole('status')).toHaveTextContent('Saved');
```

Clearing removes recorded call, instance, context, and result data but leaves the mock implementation in place. Resetting also removes the mock implementation, so a mock created with a custom behavior returns to an empty mock. Restoring applies to spies or replaced properties: it puts the original implementation or property back. The distinction matters because a test can leak either call history or altered behavior into a later test. Use the narrowest lifecycle operation that matches the intent, and prefer configuration that automatically restores or clears state when the suite needs isolation. Never rely on ordering between tests to make a mock safe.

```ts caption="A spy should be restored so later tests see the original implementation."
const spy = jest.spyOn(Date, 'now').mockReturnValue(0);
expect(Date.now()).toBe(0);
spy.mockRestore();
```

Start with a user-facing contract: a role plus accessible name, a label, or visible text. These selectors make the test exercise the same interface users and assistive technology rely on, and they expose accessibility regressions instead of hiding them. Use a test id when no meaningful user-facing contract exists, such as a canvas or a deliberately non-semantic hook. Avoid long CSS paths and XPath tied to DOM structure; those selectors break when markup or styling changes without a behavior change. Keep locators specific enough to identify one target, and use Playwright's locator actions and web-first assertions rather than manual sleeps.

```ts caption="A role and accessible name form a user-facing locator and assertion."
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit order' }).click();
await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toHaveText('Order submitted');
```

Make each test independently runnable: use isolated browser contexts and independent data, and avoid depending on a preceding test. Locate elements through stable user-facing contracts. After an action, await a web-first assertion such as `toBeVisible`, `toHaveText`, `toHaveURL`, or `toHaveCount`; these assertions re-fetch and retry until their timeout. Do not use a fixed sleep or call a non-retrying inspection such as `isVisible()` as if it were a wait. If the test still fails, capture a trace on the first retry and inspect DOM snapshots, network, console, and action timing. Fix the race or state leak rather than masking it with more retries.

```ts caption="A web-first assertion waits for the eventual user-visible state."
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();
await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toHaveText('Saved');
```

TDD is a feedback loop: write a small test for a desired behavior, see it fail for the expected reason, implement the smallest change that makes it pass, then refactor while keeping the tests green. The test is a precise executable example of the contract, not merely a coverage exercise. It is especially useful for well-defined rules, reducers, parsers, and bug fixes. For exploratory UI or an unclear interaction, I may first build a thin spike to learn the design, then preserve the discovered behavior with tests. The discipline is short feedback and incremental design, not defending a ceremony when it slows learning.

```ts caption="A small behavior example can drive the implementation before refactoring."
it('rejects an empty project name', () => {
  expect(validateProjectName('')).toEqual({ ok: false, reason: 'required' });
});
```

Treat coverage as a diagnostic map, not as a proof that the suite is good. Line, statement, function, and branch percentages tell you what execution is missing, but a test can execute code without asserting a meaningful outcome. Gate the checks that matter to risk: tests pass, type and lint checks pass, critical paths have behavior assertions, and coverage does not regress unexpectedly. If using a numeric threshold, set it per package or risk area and review exclusions; do not chase 100% indiscriminately. Pair the metric with escaped defects, mutation or fault-injection evidence where appropriate, and the team's confidence changing important code.

```ts caption="A gate can be scoped to a high-risk domain rather than applied blindly."
export default {
  coverageThreshold: {
    './src/domain/': { branches: 90, lines: 95 }
  }
};
```

First classify the failure using the CI artifact: missing await, timing race, shared state, order dependence, non-deterministic data, real network, animation, resource pressure, or a product defect. Re-run enough to reproduce, randomize or isolate the test, and compare the failing trace with a passing run. In Playwright, use a trace on first retry to inspect action logs, DOM snapshots, screenshots, console, and network. Replace sleeps with an awaited condition, seed data and reset storage or database state, make selectors unique, and control external services. Retries may provide evidence or reduce transient noise, but they are not the fix; track ownership and remove quarantine once the cause is resolved.

```ts caption="Collect diagnostic evidence on a retry while fixing the underlying nondeterminism."
export default defineConfig({
  retries: process.env.CI ? 1 : 0,
  use: { trace: 'on-first-retry' }
});
```

It is valuable when pixels are part of the contract: design-system components, key responsive layouts, and important themes. It complements, rather than replaces, semantic and behavior assertions; a screenshot can look unchanged while a button loses its accessible name. Keep the target deterministic: pin the browser and execution environment, use stable data, wait for the UI to settle, and mask or hide volatile regions such as timestamps. Review every baseline diff as code: approve intentional design changes deliberately and investigate unexplained changes. Playwright's screenshot matcher creates references and compares later runs, but its documentation warns that rendering varies across operating systems, browsers, fonts, and hardware.

```ts caption="A named screenshot and stylesheet can make a visual contract reviewable and less noisy."
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('dashboard.png', {
  stylePath: 'tests/hide-volatile.css'
});
```

Choose projects from user and production risk, not from a wish to run every combination on every change. Run a fast, representative smoke set on pull requests, then expand critical journeys across supported engines and viewports at a merge, staging, or scheduled gate. Keep tests isolated so projects can run in parallel, shard when runtime requires it, and preserve traces and screenshots as failure artifacts. Use the same pinned environment for visual baselines. The matrix should be a living product decision: revisit it from browser usage, escaped defects, release risk, and execution cost. A smaller trustworthy suite across the right engines is better than a larger suite that teams routinely ignore.

```ts caption="Projects express deliberate browser coverage that CI can schedule independently."
export default defineConfig({
  projects: [
    { name: 'chromium', use: { ...devices['Desktop Chrome'] } },
    { name: 'webkit', use: { ...devices['Desktop Safari'] } }
  ]
});
```

Use separate contracts for separate failure classes. Behavior tests assert actions and outcomes; accessible locators and targeted accessibility checks protect names, roles, states, and keyboard behavior; visual comparisons protect layout, styling, spacing, and responsive presentation. Run each where it is most stable—isolated components or deterministic stories for visual coverage, and critical composed flows for semantics and behavior. Review baseline changes with the same ownership as code, and do not accept a green screenshot as evidence that the UI is accessible. Conversely, a semantic test will not detect a broken grid or missing visual affordance. The architecture should make each signal explainable, reviewable, and tied to a product risk.

```ts caption="Semantic and visual assertions cover different contracts for the same UI."
await expect(page.getByRole('dialog', { name: 'Confirm delete' })).toBeVisible();
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('confirm-dialog.png');
```
